SummaryNodemailer's SMTP client applies four regular expressions to a peer's EHLO response to detect the supported SASL mechanisms. Each pattern uses an alternation with two overlapping whitespace-matching sub-patterns, which produces quadratic (O(n^2)) backtracking whenever the mechanism name is absent from the line. A malicious or MITM SMTP server that answers EHLO with 250-AUTH followed by a long whitespace-only run and a non-matching token blocks the Node.js event loop for tens of seconds to hours during a pre-authentication handshake. No authentication to the target SMTP server is required, and any application that connects to an attacker-influenced host is affected.Root CauseEach of the four AUTH mechanism regexes in SMTPConnection._actionEHLO contains the sub-pattern (?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=) before the mechanism literal. [^
]* and the trailing \s+ both match whitespace and can consume the same characters, so when the mechanism literal is absent, the regex engine must try every partition of the whitespace run between [^
]* and \s+ before failing. The four regexes run unconditionally on every EHLO response, with no length cap, no shape validation, and no early rejection before the engine enters the ambiguous alternation.Component: nodemailer (npm), SMTP clientFunction:  SMTPConnection._actionEHLOLocation:  src/smtp-connection/index.ts, lines 2162, 2167, 2172, 2177Patterns:  /[ -]AUTH(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)PLAIN/i           /[ -]AUTH(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)LOGIN/i           /[ -]AUTH(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)CRAM-MD5/i           /[ -]AUTH(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)XOAUTH2/iImpactAlgorithmic-complexity denial of service against any Node.js process that opens an SMTP connection to an attacker-controlled or attacker-influenced host. Node.js is single threaded, so the process stalls entirely while the regex engine backtracks: no other requests are served, no timers fire, and the built-in socket timeout (10 minutes) cannot interrupt synchronous JavaScript. A 4,000-character whitespace run inside a single continuation line already blocks each regex for tens of seconds; the client bounds a response at 1 MB, which leaves headroom for blocking runs measured in hours across the four patterns. Two realistic attacker positions reach the sink pre-authentication: a hosted mailer that lets tenants configure their own SMTP server, and any network-adjacent attacker able to man-in-the-middle a cleartext SMTP connection (including before STARTTLS negotiation).Proof of ConceptThe PoC exercises the exact four regexes copied from src/smtp-connection/index.ts against a synthesized 250-AUTH<whitespace>NOMATCH line, so no live SMTP server is needed to reproduce the timing.Runtime:      Node.js >= 20Dependencies: noneInvocation:   node poc.js'use strict';const AUTH_REGEXES = [    /[ -]AUTH(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)PLAIN/i,    /[ -]AUTH(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)LOGIN/i,    /[ -]AUTH(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)CRAM-MD5/i,    /[ -]AUTH(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)XOAUTH2/i,];function bench(n) {    const line = '250-AUTH' + ' '.repeat(n) + 'NOMATCH';    const start = Date.now();    for (const re of AUTH_REGEXES) re.test(line);    return Date.now() - start;}for (const n of [500, 1000, 2000, 3000]) {    console.log(`n=${n}  four-regex time=${bench(n)} ms`);}const control = '250-AUTH PLAIN LOGIN CRAM-MD5 XOAUTH2';const t0 = Date.now();for (const re of AUTH_REGEXES) re.test(control);console.log(`well-formed control  four-regex time=${Date.now() - t0} ms`);n=500   four-regex time=914 msn=1000  four-regex time=1348 msn=2000  four-regex time=10558 msn=3000  four-regex time=35251 mswell-formed control  four-regex time=0 msDoubling the whitespace run multiplies the wall time by roughly four, which matches the expected O(n^2) growth. The well-formed control shows that a normal EHLO response is parsed in effectively zero time.Attack ChainExposure: The victim application, or any dependent that uses nodemailer to send mail, opens a TCP or TLS connection to an SMTP host the attacker controls. This happens automatically for multi-tenant mailers whose tenants configure their own outbound SMTP server, and for any client whose plaintext SMTP session can be intercepted by a network-adjacent attacker before STARTTLS negotiation.Control: The attacker fully controls the bytes returned in response to EHLO. In particular, the attacker chooses the length and content of every 250- continuation line up to the client's 1 MB response cap.Path: Socket bytes are reassembled by _onData, decoded by _processResponse, and dispatched to _actionEHLO. On the first EHLO after the greeting (malicious server with no STARTTLS), on the second EHLO after a successful STARTTLS upgrade, or on the first EHLO when ignoreTLS is set, control reaches the AUTH detection block near the end of _actionEHLO.Guard: The only bounds on this path are maxResponseSize (1 MB by default) and the 421 or non-2xx status-code check. Neither prevents O(n^2) backtracking: 4,000 whitespace character
